:Well, here it comes, I guess I'd better do something fast, or else clear
:out of here...
     Wonderful.  I'd love to see the look on someone's face when he zaps
his wand of magic missile and receives the message "Nothing happens." 
At that point, you not only run, you hope to high hell that there's not
anything in your way.  I've had everything from floating eyes to
gelatinous cubes to soldier ants get in my way on a necessary retreat. 
Rarely do I survive such conditions.  Good luck when you're being chased
by your own God's Stormbringer.

:>And, if a dwarf gets in there, you're screwed and you also have to run 
:>quickly.  I still like the idea of putting the thing in a box.  Simpler 
:>and does the job better.  (And the box shouldn't be trapped.)
:                                 [...]
:Caveats of retrieving your weapon ;^).  It's not *likely* to happen, but
:there's always the chance, so don't head straight for the weapon level
:unless you are prepared for what might be waiting.  ;^)
     Oh boy.  I can just see a first level character finding an altar
and gleefully sacrificing a newt, only to see the message "You feel your
God has just granted some lucky monster the means to kill you.  Wait until
you have the means to handle something on the level of a dwarf king with
Mjollnir before advancing to dlvl 2."
     Don't get me wrong.  I love the element of risk in Nethack.  I
don't like the idea that a benevolent gift from one's God turns into a
potential death trap.

:I guess my overall philosophy that has guided the suggestions I've made
:is that the harder you work for something (and the more dangerous it is
:to get), the more satisfaction it gives you when you finally succeed.
     Great-- for wishes.  I like the idea that God-granted gifts might
show up on a later level in a box.  I like the idea that wishes might
show up in the hands of a fire giant.  The one contingency I don't like
is that a God-granted gift might end up in the hands of a fire-giant.
     I wouldn't mind if the box was guarded, by the way.  Creating a
vault seems like too much coding.  But it couldn't be too much trouble
to map out a future level and create a box and a reasonably difficult
monster horde (something that has a chance of appearing on the level
anyway); this could be called a "quest" of a minor variety, and would
present a much more workable alternative to creating a virtually
undefeatable (at hand-to-hand) single monster.

:Wishing as currently implemented seems almost anticlimatic.  This suggestion
:provides you with a "quest".  You get to decide what you are going to chase
:after.  And there are unusual dangers presented in the accomplishment of
:the quest.  There is also guidance available in the form of the crystal ball
:(I envisioned the ball being the only item that would actually be *given*
:directly to you upon wishing for it).  Of course you could always *find* a
:crystal ball laying around in the dungeon rather than wishing for it.
     I'm not objecting to your scheme as applied to wishes.  But (unless
your God is going to grant you a crystal ball first before handing
enemies implements of death and destruction) divine gifts are a
different matter.  Besides, all the crystal ball would you is whether
you're on the right level or not; it's not all that big a help.

:The most attractive feature of this scheme to me is that it provides you with
:an intermediate goal to pursue (early goal - get enough food, armor, exp. to
:survive the next 50 turns; late goal - find the wizard) and keeps it
:interesting.
     Great-- I just have some disagreements on the implementation.  I
note that Nethack has added in some intermediate goals, although the
Tower seems to be one of the bigger flops as far as that's concerned (no
motivation).


